### Step 1: Set Up Your Project

First, make sure you have a Next.js project set up. If not, you can create one using:

```bash
npx create-next-app@latest my-app
cd my-app
```

### Step 2: Install Tailwind CSS

If you haven't already installed Tailwind CSS, you can do so by following these steps:

1. **Install Tailwind CSS**:
   ```bash
   npm install -D tailwindcss postcss autoprefixer
   npx tailwindcss init -p
   ```

2. **Configure `tailwind.config.js`**:
   Add the paths to your template files where you use Tailwind classes.
   ```javascript
   module.exports = {
     content: [
       './pages/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}',
       './components/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}',
     ],
     theme: {
       extend: {},
     },
     plugins: [],
   }
   ```

3. **Add Tailwind to your CSS file**:
   In `styles/globals.css`, add the following:
   ```css
   @tailwind base;
   @tailwind components;
   @tailwind utilities;
   ```

4. **Import the global styles in `_app.js`**:
   ```javascript
   import '../styles/globals.css';

   function MyApp({ Component, pageProps }) {
     return <Component {...pageProps} />;
   }

   export default MyApp;
   ```

### Step 3: Create the Navigation Bar

Create a new component for the navigation bar, e.g., `Navbar.js`.

#### Navbar.js
```javascript
import { useState } from 'react';
import Link from 'next/link';

const Navbar = () => {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);

  return (
    <>
      {/* Hamburger Menu */}
      <button
        className="md:hidden p-2 text-white bg-blue-500 rounded-md"
        onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}
      >
        ☰
      </button>

      {/* Sidebar */}
      <div
        className={`fixed inset-y-0 left-0 w-64 bg-gray-800 transform ${isOpen ? 'translate-x-0' : '-translate-x-full'
          } transition-transform duration-300 ease-in-out z-50`}
      >
        <nav className="flex flex-col h-full">
          <ul className="flex-1 space-y-2 py-4 px-6">
            <li>
              <Link href="/">
                <a className="text-white hover:text-blue-300">Home</a>
              </Link>
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link href="/about">
                <a className="text-white hover:text-blue-300">About</a>
              </Link>
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link href="/contact">
                <a className="text-white hover:text-blue-300">Contact</a>
              </Link>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </div>

      {/* Main Content */}
      <main className="pt-16 md:pl-64">
        <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Welcome to My App</h1>
        <p>This is some sample content.</p>
      </main>
    </>
  );
};

export default Navbar;
```

### Step 4: Use the Navigation Bar in Your Page

Now, let's use this `Navbar` component in one of your pages, for example, `index.js`.

#### index.js
```javascript
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar';

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Navbar />
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Explanation

- **Hamburger Menu**: A button that toggles the visibility of the sidebar. It uses the `md:hidden` class to hide itself on medium and larger screens.
- **Sidebar**: A fixed sidebar that slides in from the left when the hamburger menu is clicked. The `transform` property controls its position, and the `transition` property adds a smooth sliding effect.
- **Main Content**: The main content area shifts to the right when the sidebar is open, creating the illusion that the sidebar is part of the layout.

This setup ensures that the sidebar is always visible on desktops but becomes a hamburger menu on mobile devices.

