This code creates two system-style buttons and adds them to a horizontal stack view, which is then added to the keyboard view. The `button1Tapped` and `button2Tapped` methods are called when each button is tapped, respectively.
3. Build and run the extension on your device or simulator.
That's it! You should now see two buttons displayed in the keyboard view of your iMessage app.

Sure, here's an example Rust program that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and downloads the YouTube URLs using `yt-dlp`:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::process::Command;

fn main() {
    // Read URLs from a file
    let url_file = "urls.txt";
    let urls: Vec<String> = fs::read_to_string(url_file)
        .expect("Failed to read URL file")
        .lines()
        .map(String::from)
        .collect();

    // Separate YouTube URLs from other URLs
    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::new();
    for url in &urls {
        if is_youtube_url(url) {
            youtube_urls.push(url.clone());
        }
    }

    // Download YouTube URLs using yt-dlp
    for url in &youtube_urls {
        Command::new("yt-dlp")
            .arg(url)
            .output()
            .expect("Failed to execute yt-dlp");
    }
}

// Function to check if a URL is a YouTube URL
fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    url.starts_with("https://www.youtube.com/") || url.starts_with("https://youtu.be/")
}
```

In this program, we first read the URLs from a file named `urls.txt`. We then iterate over each URL and use the `is_youtube_url` function to check if it is a YouTube URL. If it is, we add it to the `youtube_urls` vector.

Finally, we loop through the `youtube_urls` vector and use the `yt-dlp` command-line tool to download each YouTube URL. The `yt-dlp` command is executed using the `Command` struct from the `std::process` module.

Note that you will need to have `yt-dlp` installed on your system for this program to work. You can install it by following the instructions on the [yt-dlp GitHub page](https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp).

Sure, I can help you create a Sierpinski Triangle using XAML. The Sierpinski Triangle is a fractal that can be created by recursively subdividing an equilateral triangle into smaller triangles.

Here's a simple example of how you might create a Sierpinski Triangle in WPF (Windows Presentation Foundation) using XAML:

```xml
<Window x:Class="SierpinskiTriangle.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="450" Width="800">
    <Grid>
        <!-- Define the canvas where the Sierpinski Triangle will be drawn -->
        <Canvas Name="canvas" Background="White" />
    </Grid>
</Window>
```

In this XAML code, we have a `Window` with a `Grid` containing a `Canvas`. The `Canvas` is named "canvas" and has a white background. This is where the Sierpinski Triangle will be drawn.

To actually draw the Sierpinski Triangle, you would need to use C# code behind the XAML. Here's an example of how you might do that:

```csharp
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Media;

namespace SierpinskiTriangle
{
    public partial class MainWindow : Window
    {
        public MainWindow()
        {
            InitializeComponent();
            DrawSierpinskiTriangle(100, 300, 200, 0, 0);
        }

        private void DrawSierpinskiTriangle(double x, double y, double size, int depth)
        {
            if (depth == 0)
            {
                // Draw the triangle
                var points = new PointCollection
                {
                    new Point(x, y),
                    new Point(x + size / 2, y - size * Math.Sqrt(3) / 2),
                    new Point(x + size, y)
                };

                var polygon = new Polygon
                {
                    Points = points,
                    Stroke = Brushes.Black,
                    Fill = Brushes.White
                };

                canvas.Children.Add(polygon);
            }
            else
            {
                // Recursively draw three smaller triangles
                DrawSierpinskiTriangle(x, y, size / 2, depth - 1);
                DrawSierpinskiTriangle(x + size / 2, y, size / 2, depth - 1);
                DrawSierpinskiTriangle(x + size / 4, y - size * Math.Sqrt(3) / 4, size / 2, depth - 1);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this C# code, we define a method `DrawSierpinskiTriangle` that takes the coordinates of the top-left corner of the triangle, its size, and the current depth of recursion. If the depth is zero, it draws a single triangle. Otherwise, it recursively calls itself to draw three smaller triangles.

The `MainWindow` constructor initializes the window and starts the drawing process by calling `DrawSierpinskiTriangle` with the initial parameters.

This should give you a basic Sierpinski Triangle in your WPF application. You can adjust the size and position of the triangle by changing the parameters passed to `DrawSierpinskiTriangle`.

To send a POST request with a client certificate in C#, you can use the `HttpClient` class along with the `SslStream` and `X509Certificate2` classes. Below is an example of how to do this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Path to your client certificate file (e.g., .pfx or .cer)
        string certPath = "path/to/your/certificate.pfx";
        // Password for the certificate if it's encrypted
        string certPassword = "your_certificate_password";

        // Create a new HttpClient instance
        using (var httpClient = new HttpClient())
        {
            // Load the client certificate
            X509Certificate2 clientCert = new X509Certificate2(certPath, certPassword);

            // Set up the HTTP client to use the client certificate
            var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCert);
            httpClient = new HttpClient(handler);

            // Define the URL to which the POST request will be sent
            string url = "https://example.com/api/resource";

            // Define the content to be sent in the POST request
            var content = new StringContent("{\"key\":\"value\"}", Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

            try
            {
                // Send the POST request
                HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.PostAsync(url, content);

                // Ensure the request was successful
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

                // Read the response content
                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                Console.WriteLine("Response: " + responseBody);
            }
            catch (HttpRequestException e)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("\nException Caught!");
                Console.WriteLine("Message :{0} ", e.Message);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **Client Certificate**: The `X509Certificate2` class is used to load the client certificate from a file. If the certificate is password-protected, you need to provide the password.

2. **HttpClientHandler**: The `HttpClientHandler` is configured to include the client certificate by adding it to the `ClientCertificates` collection.

3. **POST Request**: The `PostAsync` method is used to send the POST request. The content of the request is defined as a JSON string wrapped in a `StringContent` object.

4. **Error Handling**: The `EnsureSuccessStatusCode` method ensures that the request was successful. If not, an exception is thrown.

### Notes:
- Replace `"path/to/your/certificate.pfx"` with the actual path to your client certificate file.
- Replace `"your_certificate_password"` with the password for your certificate if it is encrypted.
- Replace `"https://example.com/api/resource"` with the actual URL you want to send the POST request to.
- The JSON content `"{\"key\":\"value\"}"` should be replaced with the actual data you want to send.

This code will send a POST request with the specified client certificate and print the response to the console.

To extract hashtags from a given text in C#, you can use regular expressions (regex). Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "This is a sample text with #hashtags and #moreHashtags.";

        // Define the regex pattern for matching hashtags
        string hashtagPattern = @"#\w+";

        // Use Regex.Matches to find all matches in the input text
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(inputText, hashtagPattern);

        // Iterate through the matches and print them
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:
- The `inputText` variable contains the text from which you want to extract hashtags.
- The `hashtagPattern` variable defines the regex pattern that matches any word starting with a `#`.
- The `Regex.Matches` method finds all occurrences of the pattern in the input text.
- Finally, we iterate over the `MatchCollection` and print each matched hashtag.

When you run this program, it will output:
```
#hashtags
#moreHashtags
```

This approach ensures that only valid hashtags are extracted from the text.

To add the functionality to the button, you can use JavaScript (or jQuery if you prefer) to handle the click event and make an AJAX request to your API endpoint. Here's how you can do it:

### Using Vanilla JavaScript

```html
<script>
document.querySelector('.btn-primary').addEventListener('click', function() {
    const userName = document.getElementById('user_name').value;
    const userKey = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

    fetch(`http://localhost:5000/api/users/${userName}/devices/${userKey}`, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json'
        }
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        // Optionally, update the table or show a success message
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
        // Optionally, show an error message
    });
});
</script>
```

### Using jQuery

If you prefer using jQuery, you can include the following script:

```html
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
    $('.btn-primary').on('click', function() {
        const userName = $('#user_name').val();
        const userKey = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

        $.ajax({
            url: `http://localhost:5000/api/users/${userName}/devices/${userKey}`,
            type: 'POST',
            contentType: 'application/json',
            success: function(data) {
                console.log('Success:', data);
                // Optionally, update the table or show a success message
            },
            error: function(error) {
                console.error('Error:', error);
                // Optionally, show an error message
            }
        });
    });
});
</script>
```

### Explanation

1. **Event Listener**: The button click event is captured using either `addEventListener` in vanilla JavaScript or `.on()` in jQuery.
2. **Get Input Value**: The value from the input field with the ID `user_name` is retrieved.
3. **API Call**: An AJAX request is made to the specified API endpoint using `fetch` in vanilla JavaScript or `$.ajax` in jQuery.
4. **Response Handling**: The response from the server is logged to the console, and you can optionally update the UI or display messages based on the response.

Make sure to include the necessary scripts (jQuery if you choose to use it) in your HTML file.

